Here is the full audit of `CatalogTest.java` against the testsmells.org 19-smell catalog.

---

## Summary Dashboard

| Severity | Smell Count | Affected Tests |
|----------|-------------|----------------|
| High     | 4           | 4              |
| Medium   | 4           | 4              |
| Low      | 2           | 2              |
| **Total**| **10**      | **10**         |

_(1 test — `addThrowsOnNegativeCount` — is clean.)_

---

## Findings by Severity

### 🔴 HIGH

#### 1. Conditional Test Logic — `addConditionalLogic` (line 26)

```java
for (String sku : skus) {
    if (sku.equals("b")) {
        catalog.add(sku, 10);
    } else {
        catalog.add(sku, 1);
    }
}
```

The `if/else` inside the loop means path coverage of the test itself is uncertain. A reader must mentally trace the branches to understand what's being set up.

**Fix:** Replace with explicit, linear setup and separate per-SKU assertions:

```java
@Test
void addAccumulatesPerSku() {
    catalog.add("a", 1);
    catalog.add("b", 10);
    catalog.add("c", 1);

    assertEquals(1,  catalog.quantity("a"));
    assertEquals(10, catalog.quantity("b"));
    assertEquals(1,  catalog.quantity("c"));
}
```

---

#### 2. Mystery Guest — `addLoadsFromFile` (line 40)

```java
Path tmp = Files.createTempFile("catalog", ".txt");
Files.writeString(tmp, "x:7");
String[] parts = Files.readString(tmp).split(":");
catalog.add(parts[0], Integer.parseInt(parts[1]));
```

The test depends on `java.nio.file.Files` — a real filesystem resource. If temp-dir permissions or disk I/O fail, the test breaks for reasons unrelated to `Catalog`.

**Fix:** Inline the data; the file round-trip adds nothing:

```java
@Test
void addIncreasesStock() {
    catalog.add("x", 7);
    assertTrue(catalog.inStock("x"));
    assertEquals(7, catalog.quantity("x"));
}
```

---

#### 3. Sleepy Test — `addSleepy` (line 50)

```java
catalog.add("a", 1);
Thread.sleep(200);
assertEquals(1, catalog.quantity("a"));
```

`Catalog` is purely synchronous — the `Thread.sleep(200)` adds 200 ms of waste with zero value and introduces a timing dependency.

**Fix:** Delete the sleep:

```java
@Test
void addSingleItemQuantityIsOne() {
    catalog.add("a", 1);
    assertEquals(1, catalog.quantity("a"));
}
```

---

#### 4. Assertion-Free Test — `addAndDoNothing` (line 58)

```java
@Test
void addAndDoNothing() {
    catalog.add("a", 1);
}
```

No assertion, no `assertThrows`, no mock verification. This test passes even if `add` silently discards the item.

**Fix:** Assert the observable effect:

```java
@Test
void addMakesItemInStock() {
    catalog.add("a", 1);
    assertTrue(catalog.inStock("a"));
}
```

---

### 🟡 MEDIUM

#### 5. Eager Test — `doEverythingAtOnce` (line 64)

```java
catalog.add("a", 1);   // add
catalog.quantity("a");  // quantity
catalog.inStock("c");   // inStock
catalog.toString();     // toString
```

Four distinct production methods are exercised. When this test fails, you must trace which method caused the problem.

**Fix:** Split into focused tests, one per behavior:

```java
@Test
void quantitySumsMultipleAdds() {
    catalog.add("a", 1);
    catalog.add("b", 2);
    catalog.add("c", 3);
    assertEquals(6, catalog.quantity("a") + catalog.quantity("b") + catalog.quantity("c"));
}
```

Remove the dead `inStock` / `toString` calls or give them their own tests.

---

#### 6. Magic Number Test — `quantityAfterAdds` (line 77)

```java
catalog.add("a", 41);
catalog.add("a", 1);
assertEquals(42, catalog.quantity("a"));
```

Why 41 and 1? Why 42? The numbers are opaque. A reader must do arithmetic to verify the expectation.

**Fix:** Use self-documenting values:

```java
@Test
void addMergesQuantityForSameSku() {
    int firstBatch  = 10;
    int secondBatch = 5;
    catalog.add("a", firstBatch);
    catalog.add("a", secondBatch);
    assertEquals(firstBatch + secondBatch, catalog.quantity("a"));
}
```

